							<p align="center"><font color="#0000ff" size="6"><strong>Amyana's Healers guide to Moonlite's Raids</strong></font></p> <p>As soon as I join a raid, I join the healer channel.&nbsp; For our guild, the healer channel is always mlhealer.&nbsp; As soon as the main tank is announced (MT), I set a hot key to target them, as well as anyone else on the tank list (ie-ST,TT,ect).&nbsp; For instance, if the MT is Roeanoke, my hotkey will look like:</p> <p>/target Roeanoke</p> <p>Some&nbsp;of the raids we do, you only need to keep your group healed, but&nbsp;sometimes we use complete heal chains.</p> <p align="center"><font color="#0000ff" size="5"><strong>The Complete Heal Chain</strong></font></p> <p>The complete heal chain is a simple thing that is complicated...ya that makes sense huh?&nbsp; The only classes I've ever heard of being in the ch chain are clerics and druids, oftentimes with druids/shamans pad healing.&nbsp; In a complete heal chain, there is a&nbsp;pause time set.&nbsp; For this example, we'll say&nbsp;its a pause of 10 seconds.&nbsp; Let's say we have&nbsp;3 people in our complete heal chain.&nbsp; The ch chain order will be announced.&nbsp; Rufusius, Sillva, Amyana (or whoever).&nbsp; Rufusius would then start the ch chain, Sillva would watch for his message in the healer channel.&nbsp; As soon as she sees his message, she would count to 10, and start her complete heal message.&nbsp; Amyana would watch for Sillva's message,&nbsp;count to 10, then start her complete heal.&nbsp; The channel might look something like this </p> <ul> <li>Rufusius tells mlhealer(5): "111--ch-- Khaelen --ch--111--go Sillva" <li>Sillva tells mlhealer(5): "222--ch-- Khalen --ch--222--go Amyana" <li>Amyana tells mlhealer(5): "333--ch -- Khaelen -- ch--333--go Rufusius" <li>Rufusius tells mlhealer(5): "111--ch-- Khaelen --ch--111--go Sillva" <li>If you were Rufusius, your Ch-chain hot key would look something like this (assuming that ch is in the 7th gem slot of your memorized spells): <li>/5 111--ch-- %T --ch--111--go Sillva <li>/5 cast 7 </li></ul> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p align="center"><font color="#0000ff"><strong><font size="5">Other Healing</font><br /></p></strong></font> <div align="left"> <h3><font color="#0000ff"><strong></strong></font><font color="#0000ff">Pad Healing</font></h3> <p>Pad, or patch,&nbsp;healers are there to help the ch chain along.&nbsp; As a pad healer, if you see the MT's hit points getting really low, toss a quick heal on him to give a complete heal, from the ch chain, time to land.&nbsp; Don't assume that the MT doesn't need a lil pad heal, things can and do go wrong at times in a ch chain... someone could be oor (out of range) or have their spell interupted in other ways.&nbsp; </p> <h3><font color="#0000ff">Assist Healing:</font></h3>Assist healing&nbsp;works better if you have the "Health of Target's Target" Leadership ability.&nbsp; This can come in handy, especially if a raid doesn't have enough healers.&nbsp; You can target the mob, and watch who has agro, and who needs heals.&nbsp; If you don't have Health of Target's Target, you can simply /assist the mob.<br /> <h3><font color="#0000ff">Healing Over Time</font></h3>The Heal Over Time (hot) spell can be really handy.&nbsp; I've heard some people say that only newbies use this spell, but personally, I find it definately has its uses.&nbsp; What is it and why is it useful you ask?&nbsp; Well, it heals your target (although there is also a group hot) a little bit a time over 24 seconds or so.&nbsp; It's handy, unless, of course, the tank is getting hit too fast or too hard lol.&nbsp; It helps allow you to heal easier when more than the main tank have agro.&nbsp; I think you should only use the hot if you're comfortable with it.&nbsp; My favorite thing about the hot, is that you don't draw agro by using it.&nbsp; <br /> <h3><font color="#0000ff">MGB Heals</font></h3>The group HoT can be mass group buffed, as well as the cleric group HoT AA.&nbsp; During a raid, the leader of the healer channel will generally let you know when to mgb a HoT.<br /> <h3><font color="#0000ff">Non-Ch Healers</font></h3>If the raid has a complete heal chain going, and you're not part of it, then you're responsible for healing people other than the MT.&nbsp; This could mean healing your group, even if there is another cleric in it.&nbsp; If the raid is low on healers, this could mean healing a lot of people.&nbsp; Generally, the raid leader will let you know who to heal, as it can vary from situation to situation.&nbsp; When in doubt, ask.<br /><font color="#0000ff"><strong></strong></font></div> <p align="center"><font color="#0000ff" size="5"><strong>Raid Rezing</strong></font></p> <p>The first clicky cleric to the zone, will start rezing.&nbsp; (clicky cleric = cleric with epic)&nbsp; Other clerics, and healers should heal each person as they get rezed.&nbsp; I always try to announce who I am rezing in the mlhealer channel.&nbsp; I try to rez at least a couple healers first.&nbsp; I try to rez a necro next, and a tank.&nbsp; Then, I rez casters.&nbsp; I rez the melee last, because they don't need to med. I run Yaulp VI while I'm doing all this to help me with mana, so I can buff people as soon as I can.&nbsp; I always rez myself last, in case I need the little bit of mana that I have.<br /></p> <p>If I missed anything, let me know...</p>
							
